---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="The Great War"
  eyebrow="Four men, one family"
  dek="A sergeant of the Army Service Corps who married nine weeks before he sailed — his wife's brother, killed at Pozières with no grave — her other brother, a stretcher-bearer decorated twice for bravery — and a cousin the archive has only just found.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This page began as two men. It is now four. A wedding at Albion Church in March
    1916 tied together a family that had already sent its sons — and the deeper the records went, the
    more of them turned up. One is the reason this archive exists at all. One is the reason it has a
    name on a memorial in France. One came home with the Military Medal <em>and Bar</em> and told his
    family, apparently, nothing. And the fourth was found this week and is barely known yet.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Sergeant, 11335</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy, Australian Army Service Corps</h2>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Service number</td><td class="m">11335</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rank</td><td><strong>Sergeant</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unit</td><td>12th Australian Depot Unit of Supply, Australian Army Service Corps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Born</td><td>South Brisbane, Queensland</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enlisted</td><td class="m">13 December 1915 · Brisbane</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Married</td><td class="m">18 March 1916 · Albion Church, Brisbane</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Embarked</td><td class="m">18 May 1916 · Sydney · HMAT <em>Demosthenes</em> A64</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Next of kin</td><td>his wife, Ivy Miriam D'Arcy</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Returned to Australia</td><td class="m">7 July 1919</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Records</td><td class="mono">AWM8 25/109/1 · NAA J34, C34558</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Read those three dates in order.</strong> He enlisted in December, married in March,
      and sailed in May. <strong>Nine weeks</strong> separated the wedding at Albion Church from the
      troopship at Sydney, and he was gone for <strong>three years and two months</strong>. Their
      eldest son, Vivian Lindsay, was born in 1916. Their next child was born in 1924.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The family remembered only that he "served with the Australian
    Forces in France during the Great War" — the phrase in his 1936 obituary, and the sum of what his
    children knew. The rank, the number, the unit, the ship and the three and a half years are
    recovered here. <a href={u("/papers")}>The obituary →</a></p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">His name is spelled <em>Lindesay</em> on one AWM record
    and <em>Lindsay Darcy</em> on the other, which is why both had to be searched.
    <a href={u("/name")}>The name →</a></p>

    <div class="lab" style="margin-top:1.8rem">Searched at RecordSearch, 9 September 2026</div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:.3rem">His service dossier could not be found — and something else was</h3>
    <p>This page previously said that a fourteen-page <strong>B2455</strong> dossier for him "is known
    to exist". <strong>That was an overclaim, and it is withdrawn.</strong> The National Archives
    catalogue was searched directly under <em>Lindesay Atkinson</em> and under <em>Lindsay
    Atkinson</em>, and in the World War I name index under <em>D'Arcy</em> and <em>Darcy</em>. No
    B2455 item came back under any of them. The search method was verified by running the same query
    for his brother-in-law, which returned Arthur Sneyd's dossier immediately — so the tool works, and
    the absence is real. <span class="chip lore">not found</span></p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">The National Archives itself warns that "not all items
    from the series may be on RecordSearch", so this is a null result rather than a proof of absence.
    It now needs the National Reference Service rather than a search box.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued →</a></p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>What the search did turn up is arguably better.</strong>
      <span class="mono">NAA: J34, C34558</span> — "<em>D'ARCY, Ivy Marion beneficiary of D'ARCY,
      Lindesay Atkinson — Service Number 11335</em>", contents dated <strong>1937–1938</strong>, held
      at Brisbane, open access, paper, item ID 32915135. Series <strong>J34 is the Repatriation
      Commission's pension case files for First World War ex-servicemen in Queensland</strong>.
      <br /><br />
      He died on 10 November 1936. Within months his widow had a repatriation pension file open in her
      own name. <span class="chip inf">inferred</span> A file's existence is not a finding about his
      death — it does not say the claim was made on those grounds, or that it succeeded — but it does
      say that the family's dealings with the Repatriation Commission did not end when he came home in
      1919, and it is the one document that might explain a death at forty-four.
      <strong>It is not digitised.</strong> <a href={u("/open-questions")}>What it would take →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Private, 5050 · 20 August 1916</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Arthur Hartley Sneyd, 9th Battalion</h2>

    <p>Ivy's younger brother. Born at <strong>Rockhampton</strong> on 11 October 1893, schooled at
    <strong>Eagle Junction State School</strong>. <strong>His service dossier has now been read</strong> —
    thirty digitised pages, NAA B2455, item 8088453 — and it corrects the record in several places,
    starting with his trade. He was not a clerk. He was a <strong>typist</strong>.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Service number</td><td class="m">5050</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Attested</td><td class="m"><strong>16 September 1915</strong>, Brisbane, before Capt. R. L. Higgins</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age at enlistment</td><td class="m">21 years 11 months</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trade</td><td><strong>Typist</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Description</td><td>5 ft 7½ in · 118 lb · fair complexion · blue eyes · dark brown hair · vision 6/6 both eyes · distinctive marks <em>nil</em></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Religion</td><td>Baptist</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Next of kin</td><td>Father — Mr A. W. H. Sneyd, first of Toowong, later of <strong>James Street, Wooloowin</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unit</td><td>7th Depot Battalion, then <strong>16th reinforcements, 9th Battalion</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ranks held</td><td><strong>Private → Corporal (16 Jan 1916) → Sergeant → Private again</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Embarked</td><td class="m">31 March 1916 · HMAT <em>Star of Victoria</em> A16</td></tr>
          <tr><td>To France</td><td class="m">Marched out from 1st A.D.B.D., Étaples, 26 July 1916 · taken on strength 29 July 1916</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Joined the battalion</td><td class="m"><strong>29 July 1916</strong> — three weeks before he was killed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Killed in action</td><td class="m"><strong>20 August 1916 · in the field, France</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age</td><td class="m">22</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Grave</td><td><strong>None known.</strong> Commemorated on the Villers-Bretonneux
            Australian National Memorial <span class="chip inf">to be confirmed</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>He gave up his stripes to get into the line.</strong> The Statement of Service carries a
      line in a clerk's hand: "<em>Reverts to ranks on joining 9th Bttn 29.7.16</em>." He had been made
      corporal in January 1916 and sergeant after that. Reinforcements joining a battalion in the field
      routinely reverted to private, and he did — on 29 July 1916, at the height of Pozières. He was
      killed twenty-two days later.
      <br /><br />
      The family said the same thing in their own words. The Roll of Honour particulars they filled in
      for the Australian War Memorial record that he <em>"gave up this rank in Egypt in order to get
      across to France"</em> — and so, independently, did a stranger in a convalescent depot at Étaples
      four months after he died. <a href={u("/finding-arthur")}>The search for him →</a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The same page carries the words <strong>"(prev. reported
    wounded)"</strong> beside the killed-in-action stamp, and the entry "<em>Wounded in action — see
    later report — 20.8.16</em>". The family in Wooloowin were told first that he had been wounded.
    The correction came afterwards.</p>

    <div class="lab" style="margin-top:1.8rem">Army Form B.2090A · 2 October 1916</div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:.3rem">The report of death</h3>
    <blockquote>
      Regiment or Corps: <strong>9th Battalion A.I.F.</strong> · Regtl. No. <strong>5050</strong> ·
      Rank <strong>Private</strong> · Name <strong>Sneyd, A. H.</strong><br />
      Died — Date <strong>20-8-16</strong>. Place <strong>In the field, France</strong>. Cause of death
      <strong>Killed in action</strong>.<br />
      Nature and date of report: <em>Army Form B.213 dated 1-9-16</em>, by the Commanding Officer,
      9th Battalion A.I.F.<br />
      Burial — Place: <strong>"Not yet to hand."</strong> Date: — . By whom reported: — .<br />
      Whether he leaves a will: in Pay Book, <strong>"Not yet to hand."</strong>
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— Officer i/c Records, ANZAC
      Section, 3rd Echelon, G.H.Q., British Expeditionary Force, 2 October 1916.
      <span class="mono">NAA B2455, SNEYD ARTHUR HARTLEY</span></footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">"Not yet to hand" is the form's way of saying nobody could tell the War Office
    where he was. It never came to hand — but it was not for want of looking. Six men were asked, and
    one of them, his closest friend, was still trying thirteen months later.
    <strong><a href={u("/finding-arthur")}>The search for Arthur →</a></strong></p>

    <div class="lab" style="margin-top:1.8rem">A.I.F. Kit Store, Hammersmith · 20 June 1918</div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:.3rem">Everything he owned</h3>
    <blockquote>
      Inventory of Effects of — <strong>5050 Sneyd, A.H., 9th Btn. A.I.F.</strong><br />
      Forwarded to — Father, Mr A.W.H. Sneyd, James Street, Wooloowin, Brisbane, Queensland.<br /><br />
      Effects. Ex Kit Store, Book No. 16.<br />
      <strong>Scarf, Letters.</strong><br /><br />
      Per "Barunga" 20.6.18. In Case No. 1224.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— A.I.F. Kit Store, 110
      Greyhound Road, Hammersmith, London W.6. <span class="mono">D/S 35134</span></footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">A scarf and some letters, in case number 1224, twenty-two months after he died.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And they almost certainly never arrived.</strong> The ship named on the form is the
      transport <strong><em>Barunga</em></strong>, and she left on 20 June 1918. Twenty-five days later
      she was torpedoed and sunk. Three Australian papers carried it within the week:
      <em>The Bundaberg Mail</em>, Friday 19 July 1918 — "<em>The steamer Barunga, on her way to
      Australia, was torpedoed and sunk</em>" (<span class="mono">nla.news-article216455798</span>);
      <em>The Maffra Spectator</em>, Monday 22 July — "<em>torpedoed and sunk on Monday… The Admiralty
      reports that the Barunga, bound for Australia</em>"
      (<span class="mono">nla.news-article69988898</span>); and the
      <em>Muswellbrook Chronicle</em>, 27 July — "<em>The Barunga was torpedoed at 4 o'clock on Monday
      afternoon</em>" (<span class="mono">nla.news-article107563656</span>). The Monday was
      <strong>15 July 1918</strong>. The papers disagree about the human cost — Maffra says there were
      no casualties, Bundaberg says one life was lost — but they agree she went down.
      <br /><br />
      <span class="chip inf">inferred</span> That the case was still aboard is an inference, not a
      document: the form records the ship and the date of despatch, and nothing in the dossier says
      what became of the case. But a case shipped on the <em>Barunga</em> on 20 June had no other way
      home. On the evidence, <strong>the scarf and the letters went to the bottom of the Atlantic</strong>,
      and the family at James Street, Wooloowin got neither a grave nor a parcel.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The family tree records "buried in France". On the evidence that is
    probably wrong in a particular and poignant way: Australians who fell in France with
    <strong>no known grave</strong> are named on the <strong>Villers-Bretonneux</strong> memorial —
    10,773 of them — and the Pozières Memorial, which stands among Australian graves, deliberately
    carries no Australian names for that reason. Every indication is that Arthur Hartley Sneyd is one
    of the 10,773.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Why he has no grave — and it is now documented.</strong> He <em>was</em> buried: a
      sergeant of the 10th Battalion reported in September 1917 that Sneyd lay "<em>in a shell hole in
      No Man's Land just out of the old German Trench</em>" with "<em>a rough cross (wooden)</em>" over
      him. That ground was fought across for two more years and the grave was lost.
      <a href={u("/finding-arthur")}>The whole search →</a>
      <br /><br />
      The <strong>Villers-Bretonneux</strong> commemoration still carries
      <span class="chip inf">inferred</span>, because the Commonwealth War Graves Commission is the
      authority on it and its site has now blocked automated access twice. It is supported by the AIF
      Project's compiled record and by the fact of the lost grave, and it needs one CWGC page to
      become documented. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Lance-Corporal, 1696 · Military Medal and Bar</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Vivian Claude Sneyd, 3rd Australian Field Ambulance</h2>

    <p>Ivy's other brother, two years younger than Arthur — and until 9 September 2026 this archive
    had four words about him: <em>"Salesman (listed as occupation on a death notice)"</em>. He was
    decorated twice for bravery under fire.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Service number</td><td class="m">1696</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rank</td><td>Lance-Corporal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unit</td><td><strong>3rd Australian Field Ambulance</strong> — a stretcher-bearer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enlisted</td><td class="m"><strong>16 July 1915</strong> · Brisbane, aged 19 years 7 months</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Birthplace</td><td><strong>Windsor, Brisbane</strong> — not Rockhampton, as the tree has it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trade</td><td>Salesman · Baptist · unmarried</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Embarked</td><td class="m">21 September 1915</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Decorations</td><td><strong>Military Medal</strong> (awarded 1 March 1917) and <strong>Bar</strong> (recommended 12 October 1917)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Also entitled to</td><td>the <strong>1914/15 Star</strong> — so he was in a theatre of war before the end of 1915</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Discharged</td><td class="m">30 July 1919</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Records</td><td class="mono">NAA B2455, item 8087524 (21 pp.) · AWM 28 · NAA J26 M17540</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The younger brother went first.</strong> Vivian enlisted on <strong>16 July 1915</strong>,
      aged nineteen. Arthur enlisted on <strong>16 September 1915</strong>, aged twenty-two — two months
      behind his little brother, to the day either side of it.
    </div>

    <div class="lab" style="margin-top:1.8rem">Recommendation for the Bar · 12 October 1917</div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:.3rem">Westhoek, east of Ypres</h3>
    <blockquote>
      During the operations at <strong>WESTHOEK, East of YPRES 4th October 1917</strong>, and
      succeeding days L/Cpl. Sneyd, while in charge of a squad of bearers showed exceptional bravery,
      endurance and cheerfulness, despite the heavy work, mud, rain, and fire from the enemy.
      Stretchers and rations were urgently needed for the forward squads — the weather being wet and
      bitterly cold and fires being unobtainable — <strong>this man volunteered to go back during the
      barrage fire of an evening counter attack, and returned with rations and stretchers for the
      R.A.P's.</strong> There was very considerable danger of being lost owing to the poor tracks and
      the darkness. The enterprise and resource of this bearer, and his personal disregard of danger,
      undoubtedly very materially helped in the difficult task of clearing the R.A.P's on this boggy
      and dangerous road. ( Awarded Military Medal 1/3/17. )
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— recommendation by the
      Commanding Officer, 3rd Australian Field Ambulance, forwarded 12 October 1917 and endorsed
      <em>"Awarded"</em>. Signed by <strong>Lieut-Col. A. Graham Butler</strong>, A.D.M.S., 1st
      Australian Division — later the official historian of the Australian Army Medical Services.
      <span class="mono">AWM 28</span></footer>
    </blockquote>

    <p class="small">R.A.P. is a Regimental Aid Post. "Clearing" one means carrying the wounded out of
    it. The date places him in the <strong>Battle of Broodseinde</strong>, in the Passchendaele mud.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And here is why this matters to the page above.</strong> The last thing anyone saw
      Arthur Sneyd doing, on the morning of 21 August 1916, was walking out into No Man's Land with a
      bandage round his head, <em>carrying a white flag behind stretcher bearers</em>.
      <br /><br />
      Fourteen months later his younger brother was decorated, for the second time, for doing exactly
      that job — going forward under a barrage to bring stretchers up and carry wounded men out.
      Neither the archive nor, as far as anyone now living knows, the family had connected the two.
      <a href={u("/finding-arthur")}>The search for Arthur →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Private, 3891 · newly found, barely known</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">And a cousin</h2>
    <p>Searching the National Archives for Vivian turned up a third Sneyd soldier the archive had no
    idea about. <strong>NAA B2455, item 8087517</strong>: <em>"SNEYD Ernest Vivian William : Service
    Number 3891 : Place of Birth — Rockhampton QLD : Place of Enlistment — Brisbane QLD : Next of Kin
    — (Sister) BALLARD Mrs V E"</em>, digitised, 1914–1920.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The tree carries him with his names in a different order:
    <strong>Vivian Ernest William Sneyd, 1890–1919</strong>, son of <strong>Thomas George Sneyd</strong>
    — William Hartley Sneyd's eldest son, and so the elder brother of Arthur and Vivian's father.
    That makes him their <strong>first cousin</strong>. <span class="chip inf">identification inferred</span>
    from the matching name, birthplace and enlistment place; the dossier itself has not been read.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The tree gives his death as <strong>1919</strong>, in Queensland,
    with no cause. A returned man dying in 1919 is a question worth asking — wounds, or the influenza
    that came home with the transports. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <div class="lab">The five months</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">March, May, August</h2>
    <p><strong>18 March 1916</strong> — Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy marries Ivy Miriam Sneyd at Albion
    Church, Brisbane.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.7rem"><strong>18 May 1916</strong> — he sails from Sydney on the
    <em>Demosthenes</em>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.7rem"><strong>20 August 1916</strong> — her brother is killed at Pozières,
    aged twenty-two.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.7rem">And around those three dates stand the others: <strong>Vivian Claude
    Sneyd</strong>, already in uniform since July 1915 and with two years of the Western Front still
    to come; and their cousin <strong>Vivian Ernest William Sneyd</strong>, 3891, who would be dead by
    1919.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">Lindesay came home in July 1919 and died at forty-four in 1936,
    leaving Ivy with five children. Arthur did not come home at all. Ivy outlived them both by
    decades and is buried at Mt Thompson. <a href={u("/sneyd")}>The Sneyds →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
